## Financial Instruments

* Financial Instruments: Overview
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:21 '** Financial products'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:2339 '** Stock vs bond'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:2308 '** Bond: definition'

- @Definition@: a **financial instrument** is a tradable contract that defines
  a claim on future cash flows
  - A stock is ownership in a business, a bond is debt

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Instrument", "What the holder gets", "Payoff driver"),
  rows: (
    ("Equity", "Ownership of a business", "Dividends and price changes"),
    ("Bond", "Claim on an issuer's debt", "Coupons and principal repayment"),
    ("Future", "Obligation to buy or sell at a set price on a set date", "Spot price at maturity versus agreed price"),
    ("Option", "Right, not obligation, to buy (call) or sell (put) at a strike", "Non-linear payoff in the underlying price"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Applications@: financial products let investors hedge risk, speculate, and
  raise funds

* Futures and Options Payoffs
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:2992 '** Futures contract'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:3802 '* Payoff of a call option at expiration'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:3831 '* Payoff of a put option at expiration'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: a **futures contract** is an obligation to buy or sell an asset
  at a price $F$ agreed today, with delivery at maturity $T$
  - The long payoff $S_T - F$ is linear in the spot price $S_T$

- @Definition@: an **option** gives the right, not the obligation, to trade at
  a strike $K$
  - Call payoff: $\max(S_T - K, 0)$
  - Put payoff: $\max(K - S_T, 0)$

- @Key idea@: futures payoffs are linear in the underlying price, option
  payoffs are not
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\footnotesize}}
\definecolor{cBlue}{RGB}{60,111,176}
\definecolor{cCoral}{RGB}{192,69,91}
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$S_T$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.2) -- (0,2.7) node[above] {payoff};
\draw[cBlue, line width=0.8pt] (0,0) -- (2.0,0) -- (4.0,2.0);
\draw[cCoral, line width=0.8pt] (0,2.0) -- (2.0,0) -- (4.0,0);
\draw[dashed, gray] (2.0,0) -- (2.0,-0.25) node[below] {$K$};
\node[cBlue] at (3.5,2.3) {call};
\node[cCoral] at (0.6,2.3) {put};
```

\footnotesize _Payoff at expiration of a long call (blue) and a long put (red)
as a function of the spot price_
