## Measuring Returns

* Simple Returns and Compounding
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1165 '** Net return over one holding period'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1262 '** Gross return for multiple periods'

- @Definition@: the **net return** over one holding period is the percentage
  price change, and the **gross return** is the price ratio
  $$
  R_t \triangleq \frac{P_t - P_{t-1}}{P_{t-1}}
  \qquad
  G_t \triangleq \frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}} = 1 + R_t
  $$

- @Fact@: gross returns multiply over $k$ periods, so net returns do not add
  $$
  1 + R_{(t, t-k)} = \prod_{j=0}^{k-1} (1 + R_{t-j})
  $$

- @Example@: a gain of 50% followed by a loss of 50% is not a round trip
  - $1.5 \times 0.5 - 1 = -25\%$

- @Remark@: the arithmetic average of net returns overstates the compound
  growth of capital

* Log and Excess Returns
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1414 '** Log return'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1461 '** Log return approximates net return'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:458 '** Excess return'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1529 '** Advantages of log returns'

- @Definition@: the **log return** is the difference of log prices
  $$
  r_t \triangleq \log\left(\frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}}\right) = \log(1 + R_t)
  $$

- @Fact@: log returns add over time, and $r_t \approx R_t$ when $R_t$ is small
  - $r_{(t, t-k)} = \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} r_{t-j}$, so a sum of random variables
    replaces a product

- @Definition@: the **excess return** is the return above the risk-free rate
  $R_f$, and the **active return** is the return above a benchmark $R_B$
  - Excess: $R_P - R_f$
  - Active: $R_P - R_B$

- @Remark@: log returns are close to Gaussian at monthly frequency, but daily
  returns stay heavy tailed

## Measuring Risk and Performance

* Volatility
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1709 '** Risk'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:277 '* Annualized SR'

- @Definition@: **volatility** is the standard deviation of returns,
  $\sigma \triangleq \sqrt{\VV[R_t]}$

- @Fact@: for uncorrelated returns, volatility scales with the square root of
  time
  $$
  \sigma_{annual} = \sigma_{daily} \sqrt{252}
  $$
  - E.g., 1% daily volatility is about $1\% \times \sqrt{252} \approx 15.9\%$
    per year

- @Limitations@:
  - Volatility treats upside and downside moves alike, but investors dislike
    only losses
  - Square-root scaling fails when returns are autocorrelated or the variance
    changes over time

* Drawdown
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:91 '* Drawdown at time t'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:96 '* Maximum drawdown'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:100 '* Maximum drawdown duration'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: the **drawdown** at time $t$ is the loss from the previous
  equity peak (the high watermark)
  $$
  DD_t \triangleq \frac{\max_{s \le t} E_s - E_t}{\max_{s \le t} E_s}
  $$

- @Definition@: the **maximum drawdown** is the largest peak-to-trough loss,
  and its **duration** is the longest time to recover a previous peak

- @Remark@: the deepest drawdown is not always the longest one

- @Key idea@: an investor tolerates a limited depth and a limited duration of
  losses, not only a limited volatility
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\footnotesize}}
\definecolor{cBlue}{RGB}{60,111,176}
\definecolor{cCoral}{RGB}{192,69,91}
