* Sharpe Ratio: Definition
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:465 '** Information ratio'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:209 '* Portfolio frontier with a risk-free asset and a risky asset'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Definition@: the **Sharpe ratio** is the mean excess return per unit of
  volatility
  $$
  SR \triangleq \frac{\EE[R_P - R_f]}{\sqrt{\VV[R_P - R_f]}}
  $$

- @Intuition@: leverage scales return and risk together, so $SR$ does not
  change, and a higher $SR$ supports more leverage at the same risk

- @Definition@: the **information ratio** is the Sharpe ratio of the active
  return $R_P - R_B$

- @Example@: a long-short momentum book with 8% return and 10% volatility has
  $SR = 0.8$, with no $R_f$ subtracted because the short leg finances the long
  leg
::::
:::: {.column width=37%}

```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\footnotesize}}
\definecolor{cBlue}{RGB}{60,111,176}
\definecolor{cOrange}{RGB}{217,142,43}
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {volatility};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {expected return};
\draw[cBlue, line width=0.8pt] (0,0.6) -- (4.0,2.6);
\fill[cOrange] (0,0.6) circle (0.07);
\node[cOrange, left] at (0,0.6) {$R_f$};
\fill[cBlue] (1.4,1.3) circle (0.07);
\node[below right] at (1.4,1.3) {1x};
\fill[cBlue] (2.8,2.0) circle (0.07);
\node[below right] at (2.8,2.0) {2x leverage};
\node[cBlue, above left] at (2.2,1.9) {slope = $SR$};
```

\footnotesize _Leverage moves a strategy along a line of constant Sharpe ratio_
::::
:::

* Sharpe Ratio: Reading It Correctly
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:77 '* How consistent is the strategy?'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:539 '* SR and statistical significance'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_trading.Chan.2017.txt:168 '* Max drawdown'

- @Question@: how long must a backtest run before a Sharpe ratio of 0.8 is
  distinguishable from luck?

- @Fact@: a Sharpe ratio is a $t$-statistic in disguise,
  $t \approx SR_{annual} \sqrt{Y}$ with $Y$ years
  - One-sided thresholds: $t = 1.645$ for 5%, $2.33$ for 1%, $3.09$ for 0.1%

- @Example@: with $SR = 0.8$, $t \approx 1.13$ after 2 years (not significant)
  and $t \approx 1.79$ after 5 years (significant at 5%)

- @Fact@: rules of thumb for a stand-alone strategy
  - $SR < 1$ is not good enough
  - $SR > 2$ is profitable almost every month
  - $SR > 3$ is profitable almost every day

- @Limitations@: $SR$ is blind to tail risk, and the maximum drawdown is a
  better tail-risk measure

## Factor Models

* Market Beta and CAPM
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:865 '* CAPM (Capital Asset Pricing Model)'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:877 '* Interpretation of $\beta$ in CAPM'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:664 '* Asset variance explained by the market: stylized fact'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: the **CAPM** (capital asset pricing model) explains an excess
  return by exposure to the market excess return
  $$
  R_i - R_f = \alpha_i + \beta_i (R_M - R_f) + \varepsilon_i
  $$
  - $\beta_i$ is the sensitivity of asset $i$ to the market

- @Fact@: only systematic risk is compensated, so CAPM implies $\alpha_i = 0$
  - Residual risk can be diversified away

- @Fact@: the market explains about 30% of the variance of a typical stock
  - A portfolio can diversify away the remaining 70%
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph CAPM {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.35;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=TB;
    // driver : orange | return : blue | residual : violet
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.8, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=12, margin="0.22,0.12", height=0.45];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Mkt  [label="Market excess\nreturn", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    A    [label="Stock A", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    B    [label="Stock B", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    EA   [label="Residual A", fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];
    EB   [label="Residual B", fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];

    Mkt -> A [label="  beta A  "];
    Mkt -> B [label="  beta B  "];
    EA -> A;
    EB -> B;
    { rank=same; EA; Mkt; EB; }
}
```
::::
:::

* Style Factors
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:907 '* 3-factor model'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:942 '* 4-factor model'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:945 '* Explanations for premium from factor exposure'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.151_Trading_strategies.Kakushadze.2018.txt:118 '* Value strategy'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.151_Trading_strategies.Kakushadze.2018.txt:126 '## Low-volatility anomaly (p. 60)'

- @Definition@: a **style factor** is a long-short portfolio built from a
  stock characteristic that earns a persistent premium

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Factor", "Long minus short", "Candidate explanation"),
  rows: (
    ("Market", "Market return minus risk-free rate", "Compensation for systematic risk"),
    ("Size", "Small caps minus big caps", "Liquidity: higher transaction costs"),
    ("Value", "High minus low book-to-market", "Financial distress risk"),
    ("Momentum", "Past winners minus past losers", "Under- and over-reaction to news"),
    ("Quality", "Profitable, stable firms minus weak firms", "Debated: risk or mispricing"),
    ("Low volatility", "Low-volatility minus high-volatility stocks", "Contradicts the risk-return trade-off"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Remark@: there is no definitive explanation of why these factors earn a
  premium
  - Risk compensation and behavioral biases are both candidates

* Alpha vs. Beta
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_equity_portfolio_management.Chincarini.2006.txt:77 '* $\alpha$ and $\beta$: Formal definition'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_trading.Chan.2017.txt:209 '* Alphas'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Definition@: given a benchmark return $R_B$, a portfolio return splits into
  an exposure part and a residual part
  $$
  R_P = \alpha + \beta R_B + \varepsilon
  $$

- @Key idea@: raising exposure to the benchmark raises return without adding
  skill

- @Example@: a long-only momentum book returns 14% while the market returns 12%
  - With $\beta = 1.1$, exposure explains 13.2%, so $\alpha \approx 0.8\%$

- @Characteristics@: alpha is almost riskless once diversified, but has limited
  capacity and disappears with time
::::
:::: {.column width=37%}

```graphviz
digraph Decomp {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.25;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=LR;
    // return : blue | exposure : sage | residual : violet
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.8, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=12, margin="0.22,0.12", height=0.45];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Ret  [label="Portfolio return", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Beta [label="Market beta\ncheap, scalable", fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
    Tilt [label="Factor tilts\nknown premia", fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
    Alph [label="Alpha\nscarce, decays", fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];

    Beta -> Ret;
    Tilt -> Ret;
    Alph -> Ret;
}
```
::::
:::

## Market Microstructure Basics

* The Limit Order Book
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.Security_trading.Hasbrouk.2017.txt:688 '### 4.1, Basic limit order processing'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:653 '** Level 2 quotes'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: the **limit order book** lists resting buy orders (bids) and
  sell orders (asks) by price level

- @Fact@: an incoming order matches when its price meets or crosses the best
  opposite quote
  - The resting order sets the execution price
  - Priority goes to price first, then to arrival time

- @Example@: a buy limit order for 50 shares at 120.29 trades 50 shares at
  120.29 against the best ask

- @Remark@: level 1 quotes show only the best bid and ask, level 2 shows the
  full depth
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Side", "Price", "Size"),
  rows: (
    ("Ask", "120.31", "300"),
    ("Ask", "120.30", "500"),
    ("Best ask", "120.29", "200"),
    ("Best bid", "120.25", "400"),
    ("Bid", "120.24", "700"),
    ("Bid", "120.23", "900"),
  ),
  caption: "Illustrative order book for stock ABC",
  col-widths: (1.4fr, 1fr, 0.8fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup
::::
:::

* Spread and Liquidity
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:81 '** Bid-ask spread'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.Security_trading.Hasbrouk.2017.txt:545 '* Several attributes for liquidity'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:692 '** Bid-ask bounce'

- @Definition@: the **bid-ask spread** is the gap between the best ask $a$ and
  the best bid $b$, and the **mid price** is their average
  $$
  S \triangleq a - b
  \qquad
  M \triangleq \frac{a + b}{2}
  $$

- @Example@: with bid 120.25 and ask 120.29, the spread is 0.04, about 3.3
  basis points of the mid price

- @Definition@: **liquidity** has three attributes
  - _Tightness_: a small spread
  - _Depth_: large quantities close to the best quotes
  - _Resiliency_: price changes from large trades dissipate quickly

- @Fact@: the spread pays market makers for inventory risk, liquidity risk, and
  trading against better-informed traders

- @Remark@: the last trade price flips between bid and ask, so observed prices
  jump without news (bid-ask bounce)

* Order Types
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:318 '** Market order'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:339 '** Limit order'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:353 '** Stop order (or stop-loss) order'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:414 '** Crossing the spread'

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Order", "Executes when", "Guarantee", "Liquidity role"),
  rows: (
    ("Market", "Immediately, at the best available price", "Execution, not price", "Takes liquidity"),
    ("Limit", "At the limit price or better", "Price, not execution", "Provides liquidity"),
    ("Stop", "Price reaches the stop price, then becomes a market order", "No price guarantee", "Takes liquidity"),
    ("Stop limit", "Price reaches the stop price, then becomes a limit order", "Price, not execution", "Provides liquidity"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Key idea@: every order trades certainty of execution against control of
  price

- @Definition@: **crossing the spread** means trading actively against the
  best quote, and a limit order trades passively and waits for the market to
  cross

## Implementation Realities

* Transaction Costs
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:5344 '* Transaction costs'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:5353 '* Slippage'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:588 '** Minimum alpha to support turnover and trading costs'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Definition@: **explicit costs** are commissions, fees, and taxes, and
  **implicit costs** are slippage and opportunity cost
  - _Slippage_ is the gap between the decision price and the execution price

- @Fact@: costs grow with turnover, the notional traded divided by the gross
  book size

- @Example@: trading 100% of the book every day at 5 bps costs
  $252 \times 5 = 1260$ bps, i.e., 12.6% per year, which turns a 10% gross
  edge into a loss

- @Example@: the momentum book turns over 100% each month at 10 bps, so costs
  are $12 \times 10 = 120$ bps per year
::::
:::: {.column width=37%}

```graphviz
digraph Net {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.3;
    ranksep=0.3;
    rankdir=TB;
    // result : blue | cost : coral
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=11, margin="0.18,0.08", height=0.38];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Gross [label="Gross edge\n8.0% per year", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Cost  [label="Costs\n1.2% per year", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];
    Net   [label="Net edge\n6.8% per year", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Gross -> Cost [label="  minus  "];
    Cost -> Net;
}
```
::::
:::

* Market Impact
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:5374 '* Market impact components'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:194 '** Market impact'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **market impact** is the effect of your own trading on the
  price: buying moves the price against the buyer

- @Components@:
  - _Instantaneous_: the cost of crossing the spread
  - _Temporary_: the order walks the book and the price later reverts
  - _Permanent_: other participants infer information, so the price stays
    higher

- @Fact@: small orders pay mostly the spread, large orders pay impact that
  depends on size, horizon, volume, and volatility
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\footnotesize}}
\definecolor{cBlue}{RGB}{60,111,176}
\definecolor{cCoral}{RGB}{192,69,91}
\begin{scope}[xscale=0.75]
\fill[gray!15] (1.6,0) rectangle (3.2,3.2);
\node[gray] at (2.4,3.0) {execution};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (7.2,0) node[right] {time};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {price};
\draw[cBlue, line width=0.8pt] (0.2,1.0) -- (1.6,1.0) -- (3.2,2.4) -- (3.8,2.1) -- (4.5,1.9) -- (5.5,1.75) -- (6.6,1.7);
\draw[dashed, gray] (1.6,1.0) -- (6.8,1.0);
\draw[dashed, gray] (3.2,1.7) -- (6.8,1.7);
\draw[<->, cCoral, line width=0.8pt] (7.0,1.0) -- (7.0,1.7);
\node[cCoral, right] at (7.0,1.35) {permanent};
\draw[<->, cCoral, line width=0.8pt] (3.0,1.7) -- (3.0,2.4);
\node[cCoral, left] at (3.0,2.05) {temporary};
\node[above] at (0.9,1.0) {arrival price};
\end{scope}
```

\footnotesize _Price path around a buy order: the temporary part reverts, the
permanent part stays_
::::
:::

* Strategy Capacity
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:4865 '* Market impact'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_trading.Chan.2017.txt:215 '- Limited capacity'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:62 '- Have low capacity so not interesting anymore for a large fund, but can be'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: **capacity** is the capital a strategy can deploy before costs
  and impact erase its edge

- @Fact@: gross PnL grows about linearly with book size, while impact cost
  grows faster, so net PnL peaks at an optimal book size

- @Fact@: a common rule keeps each trade below 1% of average daily volume

- @Characteristics@: alphas have limited capacity and decay as others discover
  them
  - Low-capacity strategies are uninteresting to large funds
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\footnotesize}}
\definecolor{cBlue}{RGB}{60,111,176}
\definecolor{cCoral}{RGB}{192,69,91}
\definecolor{cTeal}{RGB}{79,154,92}
\begin{scope}[x=0.9cm,y=0.5cm]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (6.5,0) node[right] {book size};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,6.0) node[above] {PnL};
\draw[cTeal, line width=0.4pt, domain=0:6, samples=30] plot (\x, {0.9*\x});
\draw[cCoral, line width=0.4pt, domain=0:6, samples=30] plot (\x, {0.15*\x*\x});
\draw[cBlue, line width=0.8pt, domain=0:6, samples=40] plot (\x, {0.9*\x-0.15*\x*\x});
\draw[dashed, gray] (3,0) -- (3,1.35);
\node[below] at (3,0) {capacity};
\node[cBlue, above] at (3,1.4) {net PnL};
\node[cTeal, above left] at (5.4,4.86) {gross edge};
\node[cCoral, below right] at (5.4,4.37) {impact cost};
\end{scope}
```

