# Statistical Properties of Financial Data

## Non-Stationarity

* Prices vs. Returns
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.1-Timeseries_forecasting.smd:83 '* Stationarity: Intuition'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:121 '* Non-Stationarity and Trends'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.time_series.txt:143 '* Transforming non-stationary processes in stationary ones'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1191 '## Stationarity vs memory dilemma'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: a **stationary** series has statistical properties (mean,
  variance, autocorrelation) that do not change over time

- @Fact@: prices are non-stationary, returns are approximately stationary

- @Problem@: two trending prices look related even when they are not
  (spurious regression)

- @Problem@: differencing makes a series stationary but removes its memory
  - Fractional differencing keeps the minimum memory needed for prediction
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

