* Regime Shifts and Drift
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.1-Timeseries_forecasting.smd:1376 '* Change Point Detection in Time Series'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.1-Timeseries_forecasting.smd:1405 '* Markov-Switching Models'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:35 '- Regime shifts'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:37 '* Latest crises'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.deep_learning.txt:1055 '- ! financial markets are affected by regimes'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Definition@: a **regime** is a period with a stable return distribution,
  and a **regime shift** is an abrupt change of mean, volatility, or
  correlations
  - E.g., bull and bear markets, or crises such as 2008 and the 2010 flash
    crash

- @Techniques@:
  - _Change point detection_ finds when mean, variance, or trend changes
  - _Markov-switching models_ give each regime its own parameters and switch
    between regimes with a transition matrix

- @Remark@: a regime shift can spoil a backtest that assumes one regime
::::
:::: {.column width=37%}

```graphviz
digraph Regime {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.6;
    ranksep=0.7;
    rankdir=TB;
    // calm regime : sage | stress regime : coral
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.8, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=12, margin="0.22,0.12", height=0.45];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Bull [label="Calm regime\nhigher mean\nlow volatility", fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];
    Bear [label="Stress regime\nlower mean\nhigh volatility", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];

    Bull -> Bull [label="  0.97  "];
    Bear -> Bear [label="  0.92  "];
    Bull -> Bear [label="  0.03  "];
    Bear -> Bull [label="  0.08  "];
}
```

\footnotesize _Two-state Markov-switching model with illustrative transition
probabilities_
::::
:::

* Non-Stationarity Breaks IID
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:275 '* Problem 6: Distribution Shift'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:287 '* When Temporal Structure Misleads'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:411 '- Non-stationarity'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1129 '* Financial observations are rarely IID'

- @Problem@: standard ML assumes that training and test data come from the same
  distribution $\Pr(X, Y)$, but financial data drift
  - A model fit before a regime change may not hold after it

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Shift", "What changes", "Finance example"),
  rows: (
    ("Covariate shift", "Pr(X)", "A volatility regime changes the feature distribution"),
    ("Concept drift", "Pr(Y given X)", "A factor premium shrinks once it is widely known"),
    ("Label shift", "Pr(Y)", "Default rates rise in a recession"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Characteristics@: publications and trading change the phenomenon under
  study, and experimentation is virtually impossible

- @Fact@: observations are rarely IID because labels and features built from
  overlapping returns share information

## Heavy Tails

* Kurtosis and Fat Tails
// From: book_cs_refreshers/lectures_source/Lesson91.Refresher_probability.smd:1853 '* Kurtosis'
// From: book_cs_refreshers/lectures_source/Lesson91.Refresher_probability.smd:1864 '* Excess kurtosis'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Risk_and_asset_allocation.Meucci.2005.txt:154 '* When to use Cauchy distribution'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:80 '* CER model: test for normal distribution'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **kurtosis** is the mean of the fourth power of the z-score
  $$
  \EE\left[\left(\frac{X - \mu}{\sigma}\right)^4\right]
  $$
  - A Gaussian has kurtosis 3, and **excess kurtosis** is kurtosis minus 3

- @Intuition@: high kurtosis means a sharper peak and fatter tails
  - Mass moves from the shoulders to the center and to the tails

- @Fact@: heavy-tailed families such as Student-t generalize the Gaussian
  - The Cauchy distribution has no finite moments

- @Techniques@: test normality with the Jarque-Bera test
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\footnotesize}}
\definecolor{cBlue}{RGB}{60,111,176}
\definecolor{cCoral}{RGB}{192,69,91}
\begin{scope}[x=0.62cm,y=0.33cm]
\draw[->] (-4.4,-10.0) -- (4.6,-10.0) node[right] {return};
\draw[->] (-4.4,-10.0) -- (-4.4,0.5) node[above] {log density};
\foreach \k/\l in {-4/{$-4\sigma$}, -2/{$-2\sigma$}, 0/{$0$}, 2/{$2\sigma$}, 4/{$4\sigma$}} {
  \draw (\k,-10.0) -- (\k,-10.3) node[below] {\l};
}
\draw[cBlue, line width=0.8pt, domain=-4:4, samples=60] plot (\x, {-0.919 - 0.5*\x*\x});
\draw[cCoral, line width=0.8pt, domain=-4:4, samples=60] plot (\x, {ln(0.6366) - 2*ln(1 + \x*\x)});
\node[cBlue, anchor=west] at (4.05,-8.9) {Gaussian};
\node[cCoral, anchor=west] at (4.05,-6.1) {heavy tail};
\end{scope}
```

\footnotesize _Log density of a Gaussian and a Student-t with 3 degrees of
freedom, both with unit variance_
::::
:::

* Extreme Events in Practice
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_trading.Chan.2017.txt:161 '* Example of Kelly betting for SP500'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:37 '* Latest crises'

- @Question@: how likely is a 20% one-day drop of the S&P 500 if daily returns
  are Gaussian with 1% volatility?

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Move", "Gaussian one-sided probability", "Expected wait with daily data"),
  rows: (
    ("3 sigma", "1.3 in 1,000", "About 3 years"),
    ("5 sigma", "3 in 10 million", "About 14,000 years"),
    ("10 sigma", "8 in 10^24", "Far beyond the age of the universe"),
    ("20 sigma", "3 in 10^89", "Never"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Example@: on Black Monday, Oct 19, 1987, the market fell about 20%, a
  20-sigma move under the Gaussian model

- @Example@: Kelly sizing with Gaussian estimates (mean 0.05%, volatility 1%
  per day) suggests leverage near 5
  - A 20% drop at 5x leverage would wipe out the equity

- @Key idea@: tail events are far more frequent than the Gaussian predicts, and
  sizing from Gaussian estimates can ruin a strategy

* Consequences of Heavy Tails
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_trading.Chan.2017.txt:168 '* Max drawdown'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1716 '** Value at risk (VaR): intuition'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.all.txt:1529 '** Advantages of log returns'

- @Problem@: a few extreme observations dominate squared-error losses and
  sample variances
  - Parameter estimates become unstable from one sample to the next

- @Problem@: Gaussian risk numbers understate losses
  - _Value at risk_ is the loss exceeded with a small probability, such as 5%
  - A Gaussian fit places far-tail quantiles too close to the center

- @Solution@:
  - Use robust losses (e.g., Huber) and heavy-tailed likelihoods (e.g.,
    Student-t)
  - Report tail-aware measures: maximum drawdown needs no variance estimate
    and expected shortfall looks beyond the quantile

- @Remark@: taking logs tames the tails at monthly frequency but not for daily
  or intraday returns

## Dependence Structure

* Volatility Clustering
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.time_series.txt:1112 '* Volatility clustering'
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.time_series.txt:1115 '* ARCH: intuition'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.1-Timeseries_forecasting.smd:860 '* ARCH: Intuition'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.1-Timeseries_forecasting.smd:893 '* GARCH(p, q): definition'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: **volatility clustering** means periods of large swings are
  interspersed with periods of calm

- @Definition@: **ARCH** (auto-regressive conditional heteroskedasticity)
  makes the error variance depend on past squared errors
  $$
  \varepsilon_t = \sigma_t z_t
  \qquad
  \sigma_t^2 = \omega + \alpha \varepsilon_{t-1}^2
  $$

- @Definition@: **GARCH** also adds the previous variance
  $$
  \sigma_t^2 = \omega + \alpha \varepsilon_{t-1}^2 + \beta \sigma_{t-1}^2
  $$

- @Intuition@: the variance itself follows an autoregressive process
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```tikz
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\sffamily\footnotesize}}
\definecolor{cBlue}{RGB}{60,111,176}
\begin{scope}[xscale=0.7]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (8.2,0) node[right] {$t$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.1) -- (0,2.1) node[above] {return};
