* Returns vs. Squared Returns
// From: //notes1/notes/finance.time_series.txt:945 '* AR(1) to model financial time series'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.1-Timeseries_forecasting.smd:622 '* AR(1) to Model Financial Time Series'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:90 '* CER model: test for no autocorrelation'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Fact@: returns look like white noise
  - Autocorrelations are close to zero at all lags
  - Prices look like a random walk

- @Fact@: squared returns are positively autocorrelated and decay slowly
  - This is the signature of volatility clustering

- @Example@: with 10 years of daily data, $T = 2520$, the 95% band for a zero
  autocorrelation is $\pm 1.96 / \sqrt{T} \approx \pm 0.039$

- @Key idea@: direction is hard to predict, but magnitude is predictable, so
  volatility is the easier forecasting target
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

