* Time-Varying Correlations
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.portfolio_theory.txt:625 '* Single-index model: statistical properties'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:486 '- Covariance matrix include noise due to non-determinism and limited amount of data'

- @Problem@: correlations between assets are not constant, and sample
  covariance matrices are noisy
  - Diversification is weakest when it is needed most

- @Fact@: in a one-factor model, two assets are correlated only through the
  market
  $$
  \rho_{ij}
    = \frac{\beta_i \beta_j \sigma_M^2}
           {\sqrt{(\beta_i^2 \sigma_M^2 + \sigma_{\varepsilon i}^2)
                  (\beta_j^2 \sigma_M^2 + \sigma_{\varepsilon j}^2)}}
  $$

- @Interpretation@: when market variance $\sigma_M^2$ rises against residual
  variance, every pairwise correlation rises

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Regime", "Market volatility", "Residual volatility", "Correlation"),
  rows: (
    ("Calm", "15%", "20%", "0.36"),
    ("Crisis", "40%", "20%", "0.80"),
  ),
  caption: "Two stocks with beta 1 in each regime",
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

## Weak Signals

* Low Signal-to-Noise
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1194 '* Low signal-to-noise ratio'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:295 '* The Signal-to-noise ratio is too low in finance'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Active_portfolio_management.Grinold_Kahn.1999.txt:70 '* Information coefficient'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Active_portfolio_management.Grinold_Kahn.1999.txt:75 '* Fundamental law of active management'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Motivation@: arbitrage competes predictable returns away, so the remaining
  signal is small next to the noise

- @Definition@: the **information coefficient** (IC) is the correlation
  between forecasts and realized returns
  - $R^2 = IC^2$, so $IC = 0.05$ explains 0.25% of the variance

- @Fact@: the fundamental law of active management
  $$
  IR \approx IC \times \sqrt{breadth}
  $$
  - A weak signal pays only through many independent bets
  - E.g., $IC = 0.05$ with 1,000 bets per year gives $IR \approx 1.6$
::::
:::: {.column width=37%}

